So I've just completed my very first ever ascension, and just 
in time for JuneHack!

http://alt.org/nethack/userdata/A/Anuin/dumplog/1370738454.nh343.txt

I'm greatly indebted to the authors of the wikis and sundry 
spoilers, without which I would never have made it out of the
first room.  A special thanks to The Wumpus, whose blow by blow
caveman commentary demonstrated that you don't need to play
perfectly, you just need to be able to figure out how to recover
from your mistakes. (Which is just as well, given the following.)

I know I took my sweet time about it, three weeks and 73k turns.
This was a product of equal parts experimentation, over-
preparation and general cluelessness.

I picked up the game about a month ago.  My first character was
a randomly (and fortuitously) rolled Val-Hum-Fem-Law on a local
instance.  She's still alive, but I got bored with her about XL 14.
A great introduction, but my wizards were a lot more fun to play.

My next three characters were all Wiz-Elf-Fem-Cha on NAO. The 
first fell to carelessness at XL 9, specifically lingering invisible 
around a unicorn while having very low HP. I think I'd grown cocky 
because I hadn't yet died.  The second only lasted until XL 6 before 
committing what was tantamount to suicide, risking a corpse I knew 
was of dubious age.  This was brought on largely by frustration at
a severe shortage of food.  So most of this ascension was through
territory completely new to me.

The three Anuins were not randomly chosen (obviously) but nor were 
they startscummed, since I like to practice working with what the 
RNG hands me.  Anuin the demigoddess came from particularly humble 
beginnings, but even the ring of sustain ability and the spellbook 
of knock turned out to have their uses.  She started with CON 16 
and INT 20, but also with WIS 7, which led to some early fun trying
to find the stairs.

I had her use thrown daggers as soon as she could lay hands on some. 
Force bolt was an early backup, but when she found mithril, she
turned into a combat-wizard and forgot about magic until the mid-game.

She encountered some good luck, including an soon-to-be co-aligned altar on 
DL 3 which rapidly produced Magicbane. She had a lovely cache spot,
with another co-aligned altar three steps away from the Sokoban stairs,
a general shop twenty steps further, pools down a level and a black
unicorn one up.  Then there was the Grotto Town with a splendid array 
of tools: a bag of holding, a magic whistle, a stethoscope and two 
magic lamps.  

Inevitably there was some bad luck: no wishes to be found in fountains, 
thrones or smoky potions, and those lamps? Blessed, they both produced
pet djinnis and no wish.  But by this point Anuin had two scrolls of
genocide and enough unholy water, so when it finally occurred to me to 
use them, nine silver dragons later she had her SDSM and her combat-
casting career could finally begin.

There was plenty of stupidity:
  reading a spellbook before BUC testing it (which I hadn't read
    about, lesson learned)
  putting an unidentified wand in the BoH (which I HAD read about,
    lesson definitely learned)
  eating a cat (which I'd done in a previous game; I guess I'm not
    very good at learning lessons)

But none of these proved show-stoppers and on she went. She found a 
third magic lamp and this did produce the desired result.  I asked 
for a spellbook of polymorph (there's a tedious reason why it wasn't 
a magic marker, but it's not worth the explanation). I never regretted 
this decision; I got a vast amount of useful stuff out of polypiles 
including: yes! a replacement bag of holding.

Four sinks and a very active sex life led to XL 25 even before she
hit the Castle. Yay, my very first wand of wishing! Anuin's second 
sacrifice gift had been Werebane, so I asked for a blessed greased 
+2 Grayswandir.

And got a greased silver saber named Grayswandir.  Being clueless,
I started to worry that the game had fobbed me off with a fake (I mean, 
it is that sort of game. Maybe you don't always get what you want?)
So I tried wishing for it again, and got a silver saber not named 
anything. I was seriously concerned at that point, wondering what
I ought to do next, when I juggled my weapons around enough for 
Grayswandir to blast me and reveal itself as the genuine article, and
show that all it really needed was a formal identification.

Two wishes down, two left and I stashed the wand in Anuin's bag for 
emergencies.  Almost the very next thing I did was go "use up" 
her prayer before heading into Gehennom.  After all, she hadn't
prayed since asking for holy water many turns earlier, I knew from her 
stethoscope that her alignment was okay, and she'd been hanging onto
her luckstone like grim death since accidentally crowning herself 
during that water prayer.  What could go wrong?

"You feel that Anhur is displeased." What??? It took me ages to figure
this out: wishing had increased her prayer timeout.  He was pretty 
lenient as gods go, but she lost all of her protection.  Not to worry,
a sacking of Fort Ludios, a slaughtering of Minetown shopkeepers, and
she had the gold to drop her AC back down to a comfortable level.
(For the record, she did spare Izchak, I didn't want the RNG angry at 
her as well.)

So another lesson learned: sacrifice-test your god pre-prayer 
whenever possible.  And then the Valley.  (By this stage she'd genocided
L, h and ;, as well as elf-sacrificed Jubilex and Yeenoghu out of the
way.  What can I say, I was nervous.)

Anuin was merrily looting a graveyard when "You are blasted by a magical 
explosion!" Just how stupid am I? The first time, she lost nothing
of consequence, but this time her wand of wishing had gone up in 
smoke.

I promptly edited my config file to display anything containing 
"cancellation" in a nice bright magenta, and vowed to always carry 
a second and mundane sack for anything vital and anything remotely
resembling a wand.  I was concerned that I'd lost any ascension
chances I might have had at this point.  I got lucky.

I'd intended to genocide mimics before hitting Orcus Town to improve
my chances of finding a bag there, but I stumbled on it unexpect-
edly having not read the map as carefully as I might. Many, many, many
mimics, but no bag.  I made plans to use all her markers and scrolls
to rev-genocide white unicorns if she hadn't found anything more
promising by the vibrating square, and also thought about all the
things she could possibly jettison if she had to do the run with
nothing but a sack. But the very next level offered up, yes, a FOURTH
magic lamp and thus a third bag.  

The ascension run itself was rather uneventful after all this.  By 
uneventful I mostly mean I avoided screwing up anything vital, but
the RNG smiled on me as well: the mysterious force only booted me 
down a handful of times, the Wizard only showed up once outside of 
the Plane of Earth, and the portals on Earth and Fire were close to
the arrival points.  

"Wands of teleportation are extremely useful for clearing paths
through the monsters on Astral, especially in conjunction with
jumping and fast or very fast speed." This (from the wiki) got her
through Astral. She found Anhur on her second attempt, as you can
see from the map.

Things I'd like to try in future games (aside from other roles)
include polyselfing, mounts, and taming pets past the early game.
And conducts of course. And generally learning to take fewer than 
seventy thousand turns.

I'm going to have a hard time weaning myself off wizards, but given
my playstyle, can anyone suggest a role for next time?  Because there
will no doubt be a next time.

